// ch05 §5.12: `[this]` inside a closure names the receiver the enclosing
// lambda captured -- the enclosing closure's own `this` field. When the
// enclosing lambda did not capture `this` there is no such field, and no
// receiver in the enclosing scope either, so there is nothing to name.
// Real C++ is ill-formed here for the same reason.
//
// A single-level `[this]` in a free function has always been rejected;
// nested, it used to be silently *accepted*, capturing the enclosing
// closure object itself as `this` -- a program that type-checked and
// compiled while meaning something no one wrote.
//
// The enclosing closure deliberately captures nothing and the inner
// result is used on its own: reading a capture of the outer alongside
// it would instead trip the borrow rules on the wrongly-captured `this`,
// which is a different (accidental) rejection and would hide this one.
int main() {
    auto outer = []() {
        auto inner = [this]() { return 1; };
        return inner();
    };
    return outer();
}
